(* ============================================================================
   17. A FIGURE MAY WRAP A TABLE, AND NO CARVE SOURCE SPELLS IT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-036].
      `figure.target` admits a `table`, whose own `caption` is optional.
      The fallback matrix row `figure.target[table]` distinguishes these trees:

        figure{ target: table, caption } -> <figure><table>...</table><figcaption>...</figcaption></figure>
        table{ caption }                -> <table><caption>...</caption>...</table>

      PART 9 §4's source spelling produces only the second: `^ ` after a table
      attaches to that table. The wrapper is interchange-only (CARVE-P12-066);
      an HTML importer reading `<figure><table>…<figcaption>` supplies it.

      A CANONICAL CARVE WRITER EMITS THE TARGET AND ITS CAPTION. It re-reads
      as `table{caption}`, changing the HTML and losing the wrapper. A bridge/API exposing conversion
      diagnostics SHOULD report that structural loss.
      THE TARGET'S OWN CAPTION SLOT MAY ALREADY BE FULL. In
      `figure{ target: table{ caption }, caption }`, the target's caption wins;
      the figure's caption is lost with its wrapper, covered by the diagnostic
      above. The figure's caption takes the one `^ ` slot only when the target
      has none. A writer MUST NOT emit a second `^ ` line: after a caption it
      would become literal body prose.

      A FIGURE GROUP'S TABLE PANEL IS NOT THIS WRAPPER. CARVE-P12-035 keeps a table
      panel as a plain `table` child with its own optional caption, never a
      `figure` targeting a table. The group
      captions a sequence; this figure captions one table, and source spells
      no such wrapper.
      THE TABLE IS THE ONLY HOST IN THIS POSITION. Under PART 9 §4b, every other
      captionable host (an image, quote, code block or display-math paragraph)
      becomes a `figure` when a `^ ` line follows it; its wrapper round-trips
      exactly. A table has its own caption slot.

   18. A CITATION DEFINITION IS A NODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-037].
      `type: "citation_definition"`, carrying:
        key       the citation key as the author wrote it, without the `@`
        children  the entry's inline content: what follows the `]: ` separator
                  and the optional metadata block, parsed as inline
        attrs     the leading `{author= year=}` metadata block, when the
                  definition carries one
      and its `pos` under PART 12 §4, like every other node.

      SHAPED AFTER CARVE-P12-029's LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION, NOT AFTER THE FOOTNOTE.
      That is the closer of the two analogues: a `footnote` holds BLOCKS,
      while `[@key]: {author= year=} entry` holds a metadata run plus one line
      of rendered text. So the entry is `children` of INLINE nodes, and the
      metadata lands in `attrs` -- the same two slots CARVE-P12-029 spends on a
      definition line's tail, with `key` where that clause has `label`. `key`
      rather than `label` because `citation.key` already names the same string
      at the use site, and PART 12 §3 makes field names spec surface: one construct
      identified two ways in one document is the collision PART 12 §1 exists to stop.

      NO RENDERED OUTPUT MOVES ON ANY TARGET. The node renders nothing where it
      sits; the entry's text renders in the references list the extension
      builds. Conformance is asserted at the AST level.

      TIER-2, SO THE NODE EXISTS ONLY WHERE CITATIONS ARE ENABLED. With the
      extension off, `[@key]: entry` is not a citation definition and it is not
      a reference definition either -- the leading `@` is reserved against that
      in core -- so the line is ordinary paragraph text. A default-profile parse
      therefore never produces this type, which is why the schema declares it
      and the core corpus cannot reach it.

      WHERE A DEFINITION IS RECOGNIZED IS NOT DECIDED HERE: this clause rules
      the node's shape, not recognition or hoisting from containers
      (CARVE-P12-029's link-reference hoisting and CARVE-P12-026's
      container-local abbreviation refusal). An emitted
      node satisfying this shape conforms whichever recognition policy applies.

   18a. A CITATION ITEM IS A POSITIONED INLINE NODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-038].
      Every item in `citation_group.items` carries `type: "citation"` and the
      `pos` of PART 12 §4, spanning its optional prefix, `-` author
      suppression marker, `@key`, locator and suffix. Its existing `key`,
      `prefix`, `locator`, `locatorLabel`, `locatorValue`, `suffix`,
      `suppressAuthor`, `number` and `useIndex` fields are unchanged.

      The item is content, not bookkeeping: an editor navigates to a locator,
      a diagnostic points at one key among several, and an AST transform may
      replace one citation without replacing its group. It therefore follows
      the same typed, positioned node contract as every other content-bearing
      value in the tree and belongs to the inline profile vocabulary.

      BELONGING TO THAT VOCABULARY IS NOT THE SAME AS BEING AN INLINE CHILD:
      CARVE-P12-059 holds that rule, and the inline dispatch does not name `citation`.

      PART 12 §4 GOVERNS THE POSITION, AS IT DOES EVERYWHERE ELSE, exemptions included:
      a producer unable to place a node omits `pos`; synthesized nodes MUST
      omit it. The schema MUST NOT require `pos` on `citation`. A parser
      still places every citation it reads
      ([carve#2192](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2192)).

   19. A TABLE MAY CARRY OPTIONAL POSITIONAL COLUMN METADATA -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-039]. A
      `table` MAY carry `columns`, an array whose entry at index N describes
      column N. An entry MAY carry:

        align   one of `left`, `right` or `center`
        valign  one of `top`, `middle` or `bottom`
        width   a number greater than zero and at most one: the column's
                FRACTION of the table width

      Either field may be absent, meaning unset. The array MAY be empty or
      shorter than the widest row; a missing entry means both fields are unset
      for that column. Widths need not sum to one. A sum above one is a
      diagnostic concern for a source spelling or format bridge, not an invalid
      exchange tree.

      RESOLUTION IS PER FIELD. A cell's own value wins, then the corresponding
      column value, then unset. One explicit cell field does not suppress a
      different field inherited from the column. This keeps the rule usable
      when a later version adds another alignment axis.

      SOURCE SPELLING. A table's preceding block-attribute line MAY carry
      `aligns`, `valigns` and `widths`. Each value is a comma-separated
      positional list; an empty entry means unset. Alignment names are the
      vocabularies above. Widths are numbers greater than zero and at most 100
      in source, and divide by 100 on the way into the fractional AST field;
      any other width is an error. More entries than columns is
      an error. Fewer is valid, leaves the tail unset, and produces a lint
      diagnostic. Widths need not total 100; a total above it lints.

      PRECEDENCE IS LOCALITY, PER FIELD: a cell marker wins, then an in-table
      column marker (a native head-row marker over a GFM delimiter colon, as
      CARVE-P9-004 T7 orders them), then the table attribute, then unset. Supplying two column-level spellings for one field lints and
      the in-table spelling wins. Existing header markers and delimiter colons
      keep their tree and output; attributes fill only facts they do not state.

      LISTTABLE CELL SPELLING. Because a ListTable cell is a list item, its
      marker attribute MAY carry `align=left|right|center` and/or
      `valign=top|middle|bottom`. These values populate the corresponding
      `table_cell` fields and override the positional column value independently.
      They are consumed as semantics and MUST NOT also appear as literal HTML
      `align` or `valign` attributes.

      THE WRITER RETAINS THE MOST LOCAL AVAILABLE SPELLING. A headed table
      keeps native column markers where a head cell starts in the aligned
      column. A covered column, or a head row promoted by `header-rows` with
      plain cell markers, cannot carry that native default: the writer uses
      `aligns` and `valigns` for the affected physical columns. A delimiter
      row generated to preserve a header span carries each explicit horizontal
      head default in its origin column's colon, leaving a covered placeholder
      unaligned unless that physical column has its own default.
      A headerless aligned table emits `aligns`.
      Widths emit percentages with trailing zeroes removed. Metadata a target
      cannot spell is omitted and reported by conversion diagnostics.

      HTML applies resolved horizontal and vertical alignment to each cell,
      with `text-align` before `vertical-align`; widths emit a `colgroup` and
      one `col` per entry. Markdown keeps only column horizontal alignment where
      it can emit a delimiter row. Plain and ANSI ignore all three fields.

   20. TABLE ALIGNMENT RUNS CARRY TWO INDEPENDENT AXES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-040]. A cell
      alignment run is glued to its opening pipe, after an optional `=` kind
      marker, and is terminated by exactly one space after an optional glued
      attribute block. Horizontal markers are `<`, `~`, `>`; vertical markers
      are `^`, `~`, `v` for top, middle and bottom. A horizontal marker may
      stand alone; a vertical marker MUST follow a horizontal marker or the
      `?` inheritance placeholder. Vertical-then-horizontal order is invalid.
      `~~` means center plus middle. Any other
      duplicate axis is invalid and falls back visibly rather than consuming
      a prefix. The only valid order is horizontal (`x`) then vertical (`y`).

      A lone `~` is horizontal center. `?` is the horizontal-inheritance
      placeholder and is valid only before a vertical marker: `?^`, `?~` and
      `?v` contribute no cell-level `align`, so that field resolves from the
      column while the run contributes `top`, `middle` or `bottom` as the
      cell-level `valign`. A lone `?`, a reversed vertical-plus-`?` pair and
      every other `?` run are ordinary visible content. A native `|=` run
      supplies a default only from a head row (T9), and only for its starting
      physical column. A marker on
      a GFM-promoted head-row cell overrides the delimiter default only in
      that same column. Its colspan does not copy the default into covered
      columns. A plain `|` run supplies only that cell, including when
      `header-rows` later promotes the row to HTML headers. A run with no
      following space is not alignment: this deliberately changes the glued
      `|>text` family so marker runs have an unambiguous terminator. `lint`
      reports the old glued form and `fmt --migrate` inserts the space.

      `table_cell` carries optional `valign` beside `align`, with the same
      inheritance rule. A whitespace-delimited cell containing only `^` or `<`
      remains a rowspan/colspan marker; gluing is what distinguishes alignment.

   21. U+0000 IS REPLACED ON INGEST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-041]. A reader
      replaces every U+0000 with U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER in every string
      value it ingests. It does this BEFORE any other reading of the value -
      before a sentinel is looked for in it, before it is used as a key,
      before it reaches a renderer.

      THE SUBJECT IS THE DECODED VALUE, not the bytes of a JSON document. A
      string reaches an ingest by two doors and the rule is the same at both:
      decoded from JSON text, where the only spelling that can carry this
      character is the `\u0000` escape, and handed in directly by a host that
      built the tree in memory and called the ingest on it. This clause does
      NOT relax the JSON grammar: RFC 8259 forbids an unescaped U+0000 inside
      a string, so a raw byte in JSON text is a syntax error before any Carve
      rule is reached, and stays one. It is named here only because the second
      door has no JSON layer to raise it.

      THIS MIRRORS THE PARSE BOUNDARY, which is the whole of the rule: PART 0
      INPUT performs the same replacement on source, and PART 9 §29 carves
      U+0000 out of the content class on that basis. An AST is a second door
      into the same renderers; it takes the same doormat.

      A READER DOES NOT REFUSE THE VALUE.
      CARVE-P12-030 and CARVE-P12-031 refuse an unknown property and a deviant root, because those
      are structure a producer got wrong. This is different: the replacement is
      what the parse boundary already does to the identical string, so
      performing it is the documented reading rather than a repair. Refusing
      would make an ingested document stricter than the same document written
      as source.

      AN IMPORTER IS THE SAME BOUNDARY BY THE SAME REASONING, and is stated
      here as a SHOULD rather than a MUST because the format being imported may
      have its own rule.

   22. AN INGESTED VALUE THE SCHEMA CALLS ABSENT IS NORMALIZED AWAY --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-042]. `resources/ast-schema.json` describes a list's
      `start` as "First number of an ordered list, when it is not 1". That
      sentence is a statement about what a CONFORMANT TREE looks like, and an
      encoder is the thing that has to honor it. So when an ingested tree
      carries `start: 1`, the encoder MUST NOT re-emit it: the field is dropped,
      and every tree the implementation publishes matches the documented shape.

      PART 12 §6's round trip is scoped to `parse(x)`, whose tree never carries
      `start: 1`; it does not promise JSON-to-JSON identity for an ingested
      payload no parser produced.

      THE DECIDING ASYMMETRY IS THAT NORMALIZING IS LOSSLESS. `start: 1` and no
      `start` describe the same document, so nothing an author wrote is
      discarded, while preserving the value makes the encoder's output depend
      on WHERE THE TREE CAME FROM.

      The encoder accepts this payload: `start` is schema-named and in range;
      only its default value is non-canonical.

      This does not drop `start` generally: `start: 0` and `start: 2` pass
      through unchanged.

   23. A BLOCK IMAGE IS A NAMED FIELD ON THE PARAGRAPH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-043]
      (PART 9R R7 owns the phase; this clause owns the wire).

      A paragraph the promotion phase promoted carries `blockImage: true`:

        {"type":"paragraph","blockImage":true,
         "children":[{"type":"image","src":"/u","alt":"a",
                      "ref":"r","rawRef":"[r]"}]}

      The field is set by that phase and by NOTHING ELSE. It is present only as
      `true`; a paragraph that is not a block image omits it rather than
      carrying `false`, which is CARVE-P12-042's absent-value rule applied here.

      IT IS NOT A NON-ANCHOR FLAG. CARVE-P12-005 refuses a flag on a nested link because
      that flag would carry a RENDER fact -- "HTML cannot nest anchors" -- back
      into a format that describes the source. This field carries a RESOLUTION
      fact, which CARVE-P12-005 already publishes, and it is set by the resolution phase
      rather than by one render target's limitation.

      NOT A DISTINCT NODE TYPE, deliberately. `block_image` is the honest shape
      -- it IS a different construct -- but it breaks every consumer that
      switches on node type and it ripples through the engines, the importers,
      `fmt` and this schema at once. A field is additive, and a type stays
      available later.

      AN INGESTED `blockImage` VALUE IS AUTHORITATIVE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-044]. A reader that receives
      `blockImage` USES it. It runs the promotion phase itself only for a tree
      that LACKS the field. Recomputing it on every ingest makes the field
      advisory and re-introduces the re-derivation this clause removes.

      ABSENCE IS NOT A CLAIM. It means the producer did not run the phase --
      every tree serialized before this clause lacks the field -- so a reader
      MUST NOT read absence as "this paragraph is ordinary", and MUST NOT
      reject a tree for omitting it. Requiring it was considered and refused:
      it breaks every existing AST JSON document for no benefit, and CARVE-P12-030's
      strict-ingest surface is about properties the schema does not NAME, not
      about ones it names optionally.

      `fmt` WRITES THE SOURCE SPELLING, NOT `blockImage` -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-045]. `fmt` writes the SOURCE SPELLING,
      so promotion is not observable in `fmt` output and a document whose
      reference is never defined still round-trips to itself (PART 12 §6). The
      alternative makes the writer non-local: the same paragraph would be
      written differently depending on whether a definition appears somewhere
      else in the document.

      AN IMPORTER READS THE FIELD RATHER THAN GUESSING. An HTML importer that
      escapes a following `^ ` line because the image above it "would capture
      it" is deriving PART 9R R7 a third time, in a pipeline with no access to the
      phase (`docs/html-import.md`, the detached-caption case). It reads
      `blockImage` on the tree it built instead.

   24. A CHILDREN ARRAY HOLDS ONLY A CONTENT BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-046].
      Eleven `children` fields in `resources/ast-schema.json` hold a list of
      block nodes: on `document`, `admonition`, `block_quote`,
      `definition_description`, `directive`, `div`, `figure_group`, `footnote`,
      `line_block`, `list_item` and `section`. The types they admit are the
      CONTENT blocks. A CONTAINER-INTERNAL type is not among them.

      `table_row`, `table_cell`, `list_item`, `definition_term` and
      `definition_description` exist only inside the container that owns them.
      `table.rows`, `table_row.cells`, `list.items` and
      `definition_list.items` name each of them directly, so a `children`
      array holding one is a tree no parser produces and no renderer has a
      case for.

      THE UNION IS THE ENFORCEMENT. CARVE-P12-031(d) validates a payload against the
      schema, so the block union names content blocks only, and a `children`
      array holding a container-internal type fails at decode
      ([carve#2189](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2189)).

      A `list_item` REACHES ITS OWN CHILDREN THE SAME WAY. It holds content
      blocks, so a nested `list` is a child of an item and a nested
      `list_item` is not -- which is what the source already says.

   25. A REFERENCE NODE CARRIES ITS TARGET -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-047]. A node
      whose whole purpose is to point at something names what it points at,
      and the schema marks that field `required`. `link` requires `href`,
      `image` requires `src`, `heading_ref` requires `target`, `footnote`
      requires `label`.

      `footnote_ref` requires `label` likewise. Resolution results stay
      optional, as PART 12 §5 has them: `number` arrives when the document resolves
      the reference and is absent when nothing does
      ([carve#2193](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2193)).

      AND IT IS SPELLED `label`, THE NAME ITS DEFINITION USES. A `footnote`
      carries `label` and so does a `footnote_ref`, for the identical string.
      CARVE-P12-024 settles that name on the definition and
      `link_reference_definition` spells it the same way, so the reference
      follows rather than the other way round. `id` is the worse of the two
      names here in any case, because every node ALSO carries `attrs.id`, a
      different value entirely: an authored `{#x}` rather than a lookup key,
      so one name would stand for two things on the same object. An `id` on a
      `footnote_ref` is a property the schema does not name, refused under
      CARVE-P12-030.

   26. A SPANNING CELL PUBLISHES ITS RESOLVED EXTENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-048].
      A `table_cell` that the span walk of CARVE-P9-002 extended carries
      `colspan`, `rowspan`, or both: the number of columns and rows the cell
      occupies once every `<` and `^` marker that reached it has been consumed.
      They sit on the ORIGIN cell. Absent means 1, and an ingested 1 is
      normalized away on the reasoning CARVE-P12-042 gives for a list's `start`.

      THE MARKERS STAY. A continuation cell keeps its `span`, because that is
      what the author wrote and CARVE-P12-005 records the document rather than a
      rendering of it. The counts are ADDED ALONGSIDE, the same rule that lets
      a resolved reference keep `ref` and `rawRef` beside `href` and a resolved
      footnote reference keep its label beside its number (PART 12 §5).

      A MARKER THAT FOUND NO ORIGIN CONTRIBUTES NOTHING. The walk is a total
      function: a `^` in the first row, a `<` in the first column, or a marker
      every intermediate position of which is consumed extends nobody, so no
      cell's count moves. The marker is still published, still carrying
      `span`. With no joined text it renders as an empty cell. A marker that
      carries joined `+` text (PART 9 §5 T6) keeps `span`, holds that text as
      its children, and renders a cell holding that text; a Carve writer
      writes the marker back on the standard row and the text on its `+` line
      ([carve#3158](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/3158)).

        | ^ | b |
        + t | u |

        table_row{ continuationLines: 1, cells: [
          table_cell{ span: "rowspan" }[ "t" ],
          table_cell[ "b", cell_wrap{line:1}, "u" ] ] }

      WHY IT IS PUBLISHED AT ALL: PART 12 §5 publishes resolution results alongside
      authored constructs so consumers need not reimplement the resolution
      rules, including this span walk's orphan and blocked cases.

      THE COUNT IS NOT A SECOND SPELLING OF THE MARKERS. A payload whose
      counts contradict its markers is invalid, in the sense CARVE-P12-030 means: the
      walk is a function of the markers, so the two cannot disagree in a tree
      any producer built. A reader is not required to re-run the walk to check.

   27. A TABLE CELL MAY CARRY BLOCK CONTENT INSTEAD OF INLINE -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-049]. A `table_cell` carries EITHER `children`, the inline
      content every source-spelled cell has, OR `blocks`, a list of block
      nodes. Exactly one: never both, and never neither.

      `blocks` is interchange-only (CARVE-P12-066); format bridges use it for
      HTML flow content or Pandoc blocks. The canonical writer loses the field and reports
      `field-unspellable` for `table_cell.blocks` on the CARVE-P11-046 channel,
      as it reports `shortCaption` and a `figure` wrapping a `table`.
      The fallback matrix row `table_cell.blocks` gives the target summary.
      HTML PUTS THE BLOCKS IN THE CELL as flow content in `<td>` or `<th>`
      under ordinary block rules; the cell's other fields remain unchanged.

      THE THREE LINE-ORIENTED TARGETS FLATTEN. Markdown, plain and ANSI each
      write a cell on ONE LINE, so `blocks` reaches an inline-only slot and
      flattens under CARVE-P11-003: each block's inline content in document order,
      separated by one space where both sides contribute a token. A list
      contributes its items' inline content in item order and no markers; a code
      block contributes its payload as text, a newline between two lines
      becoming one space and a trailing newline nothing, every other character
      kept (a backslash is never a hard break), and neither its header nor its
      label, which are not payload. A block image, alone or as a figure's
      target, contributes itself as an inline image. A raw block and an
      abbreviation definition contribute NOTHING. ANSI styles the flattened
      inlines as usual and adds no block decoration.

        table_cell{ blocks: [ p "one", p "two" ] }  ->  | one two |

      THE FLATTEN IS REPORTED, once per flattened cell, as `field-unspellable`
      for `table_cell.blocks` on the CARVE-P11-046 channel. It dissolves a block
      boundary rather than dropping a wrapper, which is why it is reported where
      a lost wrapper is not, and it is NOT a render loss.

      WHY A SECOND FIELD RATHER THAN A WIDER `children`: the ordinary inline
      case keeps its shape; a walker branches only for a block-content cell.

      THE COUNTS AND THE MARKERS ARE UNAFFECTED. `span`, `colspan`, `rowspan`,
      `align`, `valign` and `attrs` sit on the cell and say nothing about how
      its content is spelled, so CARVE-P12-048's resolved extent applies to a block cell
      exactly as it does to an inline one.

      IT IS NOT A PLACE TO PUT A PARAGRAPH A SOURCE CELL ALREADY HOLDS. A
      source-spelled cell publishes `children`. A producer that wrapped that
      inline content in a `paragraph` and published `blocks` would be
      describing the same document two ways, which CARVE-P12-002's coalescing rule
      rejects one layer down for the same reason
      ([carve#2191](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2191)).

   28. SMALL CAPS SURVIVE INTERCHANGE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-050]. A
      `small_caps` inline node carries `children`, plus optional `attrs` and
      `pos` under the ordinary inline-node rules. It records a semantic
      small-caps distinction supplied by an AST consumer or format bridge.

      The wrapper is interchange-only (CARVE-P12-066). Structured exporters
      MAY map it to equivalent small caps. The fallback matrix row `small_caps`
      gives HTML, Markdown (CARVE-P11-045), plain and ANSI output.
      HTML renders `<span class="smallcaps">`, merging `attrs` under ordinary
      span rules. Plain and ANSI render the children without changing letter
      case. The canonical writer MUST write the children without the small-caps
      wrapper. It MUST preserve any `attrs` on an ordinary attributed span
      around those children. It MUST NOT discard children or change their
      case. It reports `structure-unspellable` for `small_caps` on
      the CARVE-P11-046 channel.
      A source spelling can be specified separately without changing this node.

   29. A DISPLAY EQUATION MAY CARRY A LABEL AND NUMBER -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-051]. A `math` node MAY carry `label`, a non-empty authored
      string with no leading or trailing whitespace, used as its visible
      numbering prefix and counter bucket. The
      cross-reference identifier remains `attrs.id`. The label survives
      resolution unchanged. A resolved labeled display equation also carries
      `number`, a positive integer assigned by PART 9R R5a, unless the math sits
      inside a `figure` whose caption carries a number placeholder and owns the
      number. The number is added beside the label under PART 12 §5's added-alongside
      rule; it never replaces it. "Inside" means at any depth in a figure's
      target or a `figure_group`'s children. For nested figures and groups, the
      nearest enclosing numbered figure or group owns the node. One numbered
      caption suppresses direct numbering for every display-math descendant in
      its target or children. Those equations retain `label`, but have no
      `number` and do not register their own `attrs.id` values as numbered
      cross-reference targets.

      Only a node carrying `display: true` may carry `number`. An inline math
      node carrying `number` is invalid on ingest. A label on inline math is
      retained for interchange but draws no number and registers no numbered
      cross-reference target. A number without a label is invalid: it has no
      counter bucket or visible prefix.

      Both fields are interchange-only (CARVE-P12-066). A canonical source writer preserves the formula and reports one
      `field-unspellable` entry per dropped field, `math.label` and
      `math.number`, on the CARVE-P11-046 channel. An exporter MAY map the pair
      to its target's equation label and numbering model.

   ============================================================================ *)
